Investigated the review's "any authenticated principal can start any
process" finding rather than patching it. Confirmed live: the global
PROCESS_DEFINITION/PROCESS_INSTANCE grants let any Plone-signed identity
(including anonymous visitors) read and start any process definition.
Decided to keep this rather than narrow it -- real access control is
Plone's own View/Add permission on the Bpm Proxy content object, and the
anonymous requester flow depends on a grant not scoped to any group (an
anonymous visitor's identity carries no group at all). Documented the
decision on ``Engine.defaultAuthorizations()`` and in
``backend/README.md``'s new "Engine authorization model" section.

Also confirmed the review's second piece of evidence (a non-admin token
getting HTTP 200 from ``/engine-rest/authorization/count``) is not a gap:
Camunda/Operaton's query/count endpoints for admin-management resources
apply row-level authorization filtering rather than a blanket 403 -- an
admin token sees the real count, a non-admin token sees an empty,
correctly-filtered result.
